    <p class="eyebrow">Concept · Niels Bjønness · November 2026</p>
    <h1>Quiet connectors</h1>
    <p>An idea we are exploring: the people who quietly hold strong teams together. Concept highlights gives the short version. Quiet connectors is the full article.</p>
  </header>

  <nav class="switch" aria-label="Choose a view">
    <div class="row">
      <button type="button" data-view="highlights" id="btn-highlights">Concept highlights</button>
      <button type="button" data-view="article" id="btn-article">Quiet connectors</button>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <article id="highlights" data-panel>
    <h1>Concept highlights</h1>
    <p class="meta"><span>Updated 25 November 2026</span></p>

    <h2>The concept in brief</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Some people in a team make the others better. They notice what a colleague needs and help, without asking for credit. We call them quiet connectors.</li>
      <li>They are hard to see for two reasons. Leaders often don't know who they are, and what they do happens between colleagues, not in reports or meetings.</li>
      <li>Research from sport supports this. Sam Walker studied more than 1,200 teams in 37 sports and found that the most dominant had a particular kind of captain. His seven traits are listed below.</li>
      <li>Research from companies supports it too. Using organizational network analysis (ONA), Rob Cross and colleagues found that 3 to 5% of employees provide 20 to 35% of the collaboration that adds value, and that leaders were surprised by at least half the names.</li>
      <li>An organization can block this without knowing it, for example by reorganizing, by promoting or overloading the wrong people, or by putting quiet connectors in the spotlight.</li>
      <li>We want to map who people turn to with ONA, and use AI to capture signals and keep the map current. Then we work over time with a sports psychologist and a PhD researcher to understand what these people actually do.</li>
      <li>The goal is to help the organization keep making room for them. We don't aim to change the quiet connectors or take credit for what they do.</li>
      <li>This is a long-term way of working built on research, not a fix with a promised return. It suits leaders who are curious about why their team performs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Our working hypothesis</h2>
    <p class="lead">We believe that identifying, capturing and nurturing the behaviour of quiet connectors sustains and increases a team's performance over time, because it builds a strong collaborative culture.</p>
    <p>A team with that core has room for stars, and a star can take it from good to great. A team without it will not be saved by a star. A team with too many stars who don't work together will not work either.</p>

    <p><strong>What we expect to see if the hypothesis holds:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The team performs well over several years, not just one good season.</li>
      <li>Results change when a quiet connector joins or leaves.</li>
      <li>Colleagues do better when that person is involved.</li>
      <li>Help and advice run through a few people, often not the ones leaders would name.</li>
      <li>Everyone in the team takes part, and a few voices don't take over.</li>
      <li>When quiet connectors are overloaded, they leave and the team gets weaker.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>What the research says so far:</strong></p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Finding</th><th>What it suggests</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sam Walker, <em>The Captain Class</em></td><td>The dominant runs began and ended with the captain. Bill Russell's Celtics had never won a title before he arrived, and the run ended the year before he retired. Walker says there was "a real clear cutoff" for every team he examined (<a href="https://www.apr.org/arts-life/2017-05-14/the-captain-class-defines-the-greatest-sports-teams-ever-based-on-one-character" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NPR interview</a>).</td><td>Performance over time follows the person. Walker chose his teams on results, so this is a pattern, not proof.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Michael Lewis, on Shane Battier</td><td>"When he is on the court, his teammates get better, often a lot better, and his opponents get worse" (<a href="https://deadspin.com/shane-come-back-the-no-stats-all-star-1505690302/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deadspin</a>).</td><td>The value shows in how others perform, not in the person's own numbers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Swaab and colleagues, 2014</td><td>In football and basketball, adding top talent helped up to a point, then hurt, because players stopped coordinating. In baseball, where players depend less on each other, it did not hurt (<a href="https://www.psychologicalscience.org/news/releases/sports-teams-may-lose-out-from-having-too-much-talent.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">APS</a>).</td><td>Too many stars without collaboration can lower performance. The statistical method behind the turning point has been questioned.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Woolley and colleagues, <em>Science</em>, 2010</td><td>A group's collective intelligence was not strongly linked to its members' average or top intelligence. It was linked to their social sensitivity and to how evenly they took turns talking (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20929725/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed</a>).</td><td>How people work together matters more than who is smartest.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alex Pentland, MIT</td><td>In a study of nearly 700 people, team success was only weakly linked to members' IQ. Teams where everyone was engaged and contributing were the most likely to succeed (<a href="https://ide.mit.edu/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/IDE-Research-Brief_v12-Sandy-Pentland.pdf?x41178" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT IDE</a>).</td><td>Communication patterns can be measured and predict results.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rob Cross and colleagues</td><td>3 to 5% of employees provide 20 to 35% of value-added collaboration. Leaders were surprised by at least half the names, and heavy demand on these people predicted that they would leave (<a href="https://www.pathology.med.umich.edu/static/apps/cms/news/739/Collaboration_Grant.HBR2016.PDF" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HBR</a>).</td><td>The people who carry collaboration are few, often unseen, and at risk of burning out.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <p><strong>What would show we are wrong:</strong> results that don't change when quiet connectors leave or are overloaded, or teams that do just as well with or without them. We will look for this as actively as for support.</p>

    <h2>Walker's seven traits</h2>
    <p>Walker found these traits in the captains of the most dominant teams in his study (<a href="https://www.weforum.org/stories/leadership/7-subtle-traits-of-successful-sports-captains-and-what-you-can-learn-from-them/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WEF</a>). Each trait is in Walker's words, followed by a short explanation.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Extreme doggedness and focus in competition.</strong> They keep going, game after game.</li>
      <li><strong>Aggressive play that tests the limits of the rules.</strong> They push hard for the team, close to what is allowed.</li>
      <li><strong>A willingness to do thankless jobs in the shadows.</strong> They take the unglamorous work so others can shine.</li>
      <li><strong>A low-key, practical and democratic way of communicating.</strong> They talk with teammates one to one more than they give speeches.</li>
      <li><strong>Motivating others through passionate nonverbal displays.</strong> They lead through effort and body language.</li>
      <li><strong>Strong convictions and the courage to stand apart.</strong> They will disagree, even with the coach, when they think it matters.</li>
      <li><strong>Ironclad emotional control.</strong> They stay steady under pressure.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Not all of these are quiet. Traits 2 and 6 describe people who push and disagree. "Quiet" describes how they work with their teammates, not their personality.</p>

    <h2>How Moneyball actually worked</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The assistant was real, but the film changed him.</strong> Peter Brand in the film is fictional. He is based on Paul DePodesta, Billy Beane's assistant general manager, a Harvard graduate who had worked as a scout. DePodesta asked not to have his name used (<a href="https://www.nbcsports.com/mlb/news/paul-depodesta-explains-why-he-didnt-want-his-name-used-in-moneyball" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NBC Sports</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>They started from the goal and worked backwards.</strong> The A's set about 95 wins as the target for making the playoffs. They estimated that this needed about 135 more runs scored than allowed over a season. Then they asked which statistics actually predict runs. On-base percentage mattered more than the market was paying for (<a href="https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-071-the-analytics-edge-spring-2017/9573ac589ee65559695bb7ef1962866b_MIT15_071S17_Unit2_Moneyball.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT course material</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>The math was simple.</strong> An MIT course recreates it with linear regression on historical team data. The A's built their own metrics, but the core idea was to test common beliefs against results.</li>
      <li><strong>They knew the limits.</strong> DePodesta: "The guiding principle of our work is figuring out a way to deal with uncertainty." They also looked at confidence and at how players handle teammates, coaches and adversity (<a href="https://nautil.us/revisiting-moneyball-with-paul-depodesta-236084" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nautilus</a>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Moneyball relates to ONA</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The same idea.</strong> Both look past what is visible to find what actually drives results.</li>
      <li><strong>A different object.</strong> Moneyball counted what individual players did in games. ONA maps relationships: who turns to whom. A quiet connector's value lies mostly in the relationships.</li>
      <li><strong>The bridge is Shane Battier.</strong> In a later article, Lewis describes how the Houston Rockets valued Battier: "When he is on the court, his teammates get better, often a lot better, and his opponents get worse" (<a href="https://deadspin.com/shane-come-back-the-no-stats-all-star-1505690302/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deadspin</a>). That measures a person by the effect on others, which is close to what we want to do.</li>
      <li><strong>How we can use it.</strong> Start from what the team needs to achieve and work back to what drives it. Look at how the team does when certain people are involved, alongside the ONA map of who people turn to. Neither shows what the person actually does. That takes people and time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Open questions</h2>
    <p class="lead">For peers: tell us where you disagree.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Who we are.</strong> Does the description of the team and why we are doing this convince you?</li>
      <li><strong>What does the client get in the first three months?</strong> A map, a first account of what the quiet connectors do, something else?</li>
      <li><strong>The name.</strong> "Quiet connector" has stuck. Does it work for you, or does "quiet" sound like a personality type?</li>
      <li><strong>Our team.</strong> The PhD researcher, the sports psychologist and the engineer appear without names. Should they be named?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Claims still to check before anything goes out:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>That Operators has used the Attacking Vikings concept, and how the alpine team shares course information (from Niels, no written source yet).</li>
      <li>The critique of the too-much-talent study's method (seen in search results, not yet read).</li>
    </ul>
