  <article id="article" data-panel hidden>
    <h1>Quiet connectors</h1>
    <p class="meta"><span>25 November 2026</span><span>Niels Bjønness</span></p>

    <p class="lead">Many strong teams seem to have something that is hard to point to. Often it comes from a few people who see what a colleague needs and help, quietly and without asking for credit. We call them quiet connectors.</p>
    <p>We are curious about what they do, why it works, and how an organization can make room for it. This article explains where the idea comes from, what research from sport and from companies says, and how we would like to work.</p>

    <h2>A captain the team leaned on</h2>
    <p>Carles Puyol captained FC Barcelona for ten years. His teammates chose him for the job at the start of the 2004-05 season, and that season ended with the league title. He was not the star. Xavi called him "the best professional I have ever shared a dressing room with, by far." Gerard Piqué said that by Puyol's side he felt protected. One teammate called him "a pain in the arse," and added: "that's exactly what we need." In 2011, weeks after Eric Abidal had a liver transplant, Puyol invited him to lift the Champions League trophy (<a href="https://global.espn.com/football/story/_/id/37399014/all-greatest-captain-carles-puyol-inspiring-leader" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ESPN</a>).</p>
    <p>We think Puyol is a good example of a quiet connector. The team looked to the stars, but it leaned on him.</p>

    <h2>What sport has found</h2>
    <p><strong>The Captain Class.</strong> Sam Walker, a Wall Street Journal journalist, spent more than ten years studying over 1,200 teams in 37 sports (<a href="https://www.penguin.com.au/books/the-captain-class-9780670078493/extracts/1568-the-captain-class" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penguin</a>). He picked out the 16 most dominant, among them the Boston Celtics, the New Zealand All Blacks and Barcelona in 2008-13 (<a href="https://www.penguin.co.nz/books/the-captain-class-9780670078493/article/1545-identifying-best" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penguin</a>). What set them apart was not payroll, the coach or the biggest star. It was a particular kind of captain, often a defensive or support player and rarely the face of the team. Walker describes seven traits these captains share (<a href="https://www.weforum.org/stories/leadership/7-subtle-traits-of-successful-sports-captains-and-what-you-can-learn-from-them/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WEF</a>):</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Extreme doggedness and focus in competition.</li>
      <li>Aggressive play that tests the limits of the rules.</li>
      <li>A willingness to do thankless jobs in the shadows.</li>
      <li>A low-key, practical and democratic way of communicating.</li>
      <li>Motivating others through passionate nonverbal displays.</li>
      <li>Strong convictions and the courage to stand apart.</li>
      <li>Ironclad emotional control.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Not all of these traits are quiet. Some captains pushed hard and disagreed with their coach. "Quiet" describes how they work with their teammates, not their personality. Walker also says that the dominant runs began and ended with the captain. Bill Russell's Celtics had never won a title before he arrived, and their run ended the year before he retired (<a href="https://www.apr.org/arts-life/2017-05-14/the-captain-class-defines-the-greatest-sports-teams-ever-based-on-one-character" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NPR interview</a>). Walker chose his teams on their results, so this shows a pattern, not proof.</p>
    <p><strong>Moneyball.</strong> Michael Lewis's book shows that value can sit in plain sight when nobody looks for it. In 2002 the Oakland A's had a payroll of $44 million, against the New York Yankees' more than $125 million (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moneyball:_The_Art_of_Winning_an_Unfair_Game" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>). General manager Billy Beane and his assistant Paul DePodesta could not outspend the richer teams. The assistant in the film, Peter Brand, is a fictional character based on DePodesta (<a href="https://www.nbcsports.com/mlb/news/paul-depodesta-explains-why-he-didnt-want-his-name-used-in-moneyball" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NBC Sports</a>).</p>
    <p>They started from the goal. About 95 wins would take them to the playoffs, which they estimated needed about 135 more runs scored than allowed over a season. Then they asked which statistics actually predict runs. On-base percentage turned out to matter more than other teams were paying for. The math was simple, and an MIT course recreates it with linear regression (<a href="https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/15-071-the-analytics-edge-spring-2017/9573ac589ee65559695bb7ef1962866b_MIT15_071S17_Unit2_Moneyball.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT</a>). They also looked beyond the numbers, at players' confidence and at how they handled teammates and adversity. DePodesta: "The guiding principle of our work is figuring out a way to deal with uncertainty" (<a href="https://nautil.us/revisiting-moneyball-with-paul-depodesta-236084" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nautilus</a>).</p>
    <p>Moneyball measured what each player did. Later, Lewis wrote about the basketball player Shane Battier, whose value showed in others. "When he is on the court, his teammates get better, often a lot better, and his opponents get worse" (<a href="https://deadspin.com/shane-come-back-the-no-stats-all-star-1505690302/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deadspin</a>). That is closer to what quiet connectors do.</p>

    <h2>The general manager decides who is on the team</h2>
    <p>In Moneyball the central figure is the general manager, not the coach. The general manager recruits players, keeps them and lets them go. A quiet connector only stays on a team if that person sees the value, either by keeping them or by spotting them in another team and bringing them in.</p>
    <p>Two things create the value together: who is on the team, and who carries the culture. The general manager decides the first. The quiet connector carries the second.</p>
    <p>This does not translate directly to a company. The coach is closest to the visionary leader who speaks at the all-hands meeting. The general manager is the CEO or COO, depending on how they split responsibility, and usually whoever is in charge of the squad. That person also decides whether the team may be studied, and whether we are let in.</p>

    <h2>What companies have found</h2>
    <p>In companies, Rob Cross is one of the best-known researchers behind organizational network analysis (ONA), which maps who people actually turn to for advice and help. Across more than 300 organizations, he and his colleagues found that 3 to 5% of employees provide 20 to 35% of the collaboration that adds value. When the analysis named these people, leaders were "typically surprised by at least half the names on their lists." The colleagues most in demand also had the lowest engagement, and heavy demand on them predicted that they would leave (<a href="https://www.pathology.med.umich.edu/static/apps/cms/news/739/Collaboration_Grant.HBR2016.PDF" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HBR</a>).</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Sport</th><th>Companies</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>What gets overvalued</td><td>Flashy scorers and vocal stars</td><td>Charismatic presenters and titles</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What gets overlooked</td><td>On-base percentage, support players</td><td>Who people go to for advice, who bridges teams</td></tr>
        <tr><td>How it is found</td><td>Statistics and long study of teams</td><td>Peer surveys and collaboration data</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The hidden role</td><td>The quiet captain</td><td>The central connector</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>ONA collects data in two ways: short peer surveys, such as "Who do you go to when the path forward is unclear?", and data on who communicates with whom, without reading the content. It tends to show a few informal roles: central connectors inside a team, boundary spanners who link teams, and energizers who lift the people around them (<a href="https://positiveorgs.bus.umich.edu/articles/what-creates-energy-in-organizations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cross, Baker and Parker</a>).</p>
    <p>This kind of influence is granted by colleagues, not by the org chart. A study presented by researchers at the University of Missouri describes informal leadership as "continually granted" by peers. Informal leaders sit at the center of their group's advice network, and being asked for advice keeps them there (<a href="https://business.missouri.edu/sites/default/files/2026-02/THE_LEAD_F25_FINAL.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of Missouri</a>). Our reading is that when a manager leaves, the org chart changes but the advice network stays.</p>
    <p>Quiet carriers are not only found in sport and business. In <em>Who Is Government?</em>, Michael Lewis asked six writers to each profile someone doing important work for the US federal government. The publisher describes them as "universally reluctant to take credit" (<a href="https://www.penguin.co.nz/books/who-is-government-9780241778876" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penguin</a>).</p>

    <h2>Why collaboration may matter more than talent</h2>
    <p>Chemistry is the part of a team that is easiest to overlook and easiest to call impossible to measure. Several studies suggest that it can be studied, and that it matters:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>In football and basketball, adding top talent helped up to a point and then hurt, because players stopped coordinating. In baseball, where players depend less on each other, it did not hurt. The statistical method behind the turning point has been questioned (<a href="https://www.psychologicalscience.org/news/releases/sports-teams-may-lose-out-from-having-too-much-talent.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Swaab and colleagues, 2014</a>).</li>
      <li>A group's collective intelligence was not strongly linked to its members' average or top intelligence. It was linked to their social sensitivity and to how evenly they took turns talking (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20929725/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Woolley and colleagues, 2010</a>).</li>
      <li>In a study of nearly 700 people, team success was only weakly linked to members' IQ. Teams where everyone was engaged and contributing were the most likely to succeed (<a href="https://ide.mit.edu/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/IDE-Research-Brief_v12-Sandy-Pentland.pdf?x41178" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pentland, MIT</a>).</li>
      <li>Gain Line Analytics, which works with sports teams, measures "the objective level of understanding between players" and argues that this shared understanding can matter more than talent alone (<a href="https://sloanreview.mit.edu/audio/talent-versus-teamwork" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT Sloan Management Review</a>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Our working hypothesis</h2>
    <p>We believe that identifying, capturing and nurturing the behaviour of quiet connectors sustains and increases a team's performance over time, because it builds a strong collaborative culture. A team with that core has room for stars, and a star can take it from good to great. A team without it will not be saved by a star, and a team with too many stars who don't work together will not work either.</p>
    <p>If this holds, we expect to see teams that perform well over several years, results that change when a quiet connector joins or leaves, and colleagues who do better when that person is involved. We would be wrong if results don't change when quiet connectors leave or are overloaded. We will look for that as actively as for support.</p>

    <h2>What we are curious about</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>What do quiet connectors actually do, day to day, that makes a difference?</li>
      <li>Does a team that performs well know why? Could a reorg, a promotion or a heavier workload block something that works, without anyone noticing?</li>
      <li>Would more attention help them, or take away what makes them work? We suspect the spotlight could hurt, but we don't know.</li>
      <li>How can an organization be set up so it doesn't get in their way?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we would work</h2>
    <p>Walker spent ten years on his study, and Lewis followed the A's closely through a season. We want to work the same way: close to a few teams, over time. In Norway, we want to find one or more teams that already perform well and want to explore this with us.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Map.</strong> The groundwork is ONA. Open, opt-in peer surveys show who people turn to. AI captures signals from the tools the team already uses and keeps the picture current, so we don't need to interview the whole organization. A forward deployed engineer builds this into the team's own tools. We don't read private messages, and we don't infer emotions from voice, which the EU AI Act bans at work (<a href="https://legalblogs.wolterskluwer.com/global-workplace-law-and-policy/the-prohibition-of-ai-emotion-recognition-technologies-in-the-workplace-under-the-ai-act/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolters Kluwer</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Understand.</strong> A map shows where to look, not what is going on, and run once it is easily misread. A sports psychologist who works with Norwegian teams and a PhD researcher who studies founder-led companies help us study what the quiet connectors actually do, in conversations and over time.</li>
      <li><strong>Protect and strengthen.</strong> We look at what supports this behaviour and what blocks it: overload, structure, how people are promoted. Coaching can strengthen it where it helps. In one study, 20 leaders in an organization of 225 were coached, and the people closest to them in the network were more likely to see their wellbeing improve (<a href="https://ses.library.usyd.edu.au/handle/2123/9387" target="_blank" rel="noopener">O'Connor and Cavanagh, 2013</a>). In a small experiment, feedback from a person improved performance more than the same feedback from a computer (<a href="https://behavioristbookclub.com/aba-research/practitioner-development/continuous-performance-feedback--investigating-the-effects-of-feedback/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giamos and colleagues, 2024</a>).</li>
    </ol>
    <p>People come first, and AI is a tool. Many companies put AI to work and wait for results. A widely cited MIT report found that only about 5% of enterprise AI pilots produced rapid revenue growth. The report rests on interviews and surveys, so the number should be read loosely (<a href="https://fortune.com/2025/08/18/mit-report-95-percent-generative-ai-pilots-at-companies-failing-cfo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fortune</a>). We turn it around: we start with the people who carry the team, and use AI to see them better.</p>
    <p>We would start small, with one team, ideally one that is important or about to go through a big change.</p>

    <h2>Learning from sport is not new</h2>
    <p>Operators (formerly Venture Summits) brings C-suite leaders from venture companies together to share experiences, with roundtables built on peer learning (<a href="https://luma.com/szfqjyqa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luma</a>). They have used a concept from the Norwegian alpine ski team, the Attacking Vikings, where athletes share what they learn about the course with teammates still waiting to race. The skier Kjetil Jansrud has said: "There are no secrets in terms of individual tactics or techniques on the team; we share everything we know" (<a href="https://www.pulse.ng/entertainment-the-secret-of-norways-alpine-success-no-jerks-allowed/z41jcb2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pulse</a>). That is not the same as quiet connectors, but it shows that ideas from top sport can work in companies.</p>

    <h2>Risks we take seriously</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Overload.</strong> The people others lean on are often the most stretched. Their capacity has to be protected and their work recognized.</li>
      <li><strong>The spotlight.</strong> Pushing a quiet connector into a stage-facing role could cost the peer trust that made them effective. That is our reading of Walker, not a tested finding.</li>
      <li><strong>Consent and trust.</strong> People need to know what is measured and agree to it, and trust what happens with it.</li>
      <li><strong>Reading too much into the data.</strong> Walker's findings are qualitative and his teams were chosen on results. A network map shows connections, not why they exist. We don't claim to have the answers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>Organizations that already perform, but maybe not at the level they want or could, and often at a point where the pressure is on. Leaders who are curious about why their team performs, and who share our belief that this is worth exploring. This is not a promise of a set return. It is a way of working, built on a belief that research backs.</p>

    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p>Four people who share the curiosity: a business strategist and builder (me), a forward deployed engineer, a sports psychologist and a PhD researcher on founder-led companies. I have helped build data infrastructure and tools for shared knowledge in early-stage venture companies. Each time, the real challenge was the people: how they use what is built, and who has the influence to understand what the team needs.</p>

    <h2>What it takes</h2>
    <p>Consent from the people involved, trust, a general manager who takes part, and at least six months. This is a long-term commitment, not a quick fix, and it is not for everyone. We want to find out, together with teams willing to look.</p>

